# Docker build-context exclusions.
#
# # Why this file matters more here than in a typical project
#
# The build context is the repository root, and that tree contains **17 GB** of
# Windows build output:
#
#   target/        12,500 MB
#   fuzz/target/    4,544 MB
#
# Without this file every `docker build` would send all of it to the daemon. On
# Docker Desktop that is a multi-minute stall before the build even starts, and
# the `COPY` of a Linux-incompatible artifact set into an image is worse than slow
# — it is exactly the cross-platform contamination the bridge exists to prevent.
#
# The exclusions are grouped by *reason*, because a list of paths with no reasons
# is a list where nobody can tell whether an entry is still needed.

# -- Build output, which is platform-specific ---------------------------------
#
# Windows `.rlib`/`.exe`/`.pdb` files are not usable by a Linux target, and copying
# them into the image would make `cargo` skip rebuilds it must perform.
target/
fuzz/target/

# -- VCS metadata --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `.git` is the largest single directory in many repositories, and the container
# reads provenance through the **bind mount**, not through the image. Excluding it
# from the build context costs nothing and saves the transfer.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# -- Fuzzing artifacts ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Crash artifacts are the *output* of a run and belong in the results volume
# (`/results`), never baked into an image. A corpus that shipped inside an image
# would be frozen at build time and stop accumulating coverage.
fuzz/artifacts/
fuzz/corpus/

# -- Local scratch and tooling -------------------------------------------------
#
# `.scratch/` holds verification backups and injection scripts — dozens of files
# created and deleted within a session, none of which belong in an image.
.scratch/
.tools/

# -- Editor and OS noise -------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# -- Node, which QQQ does not use today but will for the npm surface ------------
#
# `node_modules/` is the canonical example of a directory that must never enter a
# build context — it is large, platform-specific, and regenerable.
node_modules/

# -- The review document that prompted this bridge -----------------------------
#
# It is a conversation export kept locally and deliberately untracked (see
# `.gitignore`). Excluding it here is belt-and-braces so a stray `docker build`
# cannot bake it into a published image.
docs/Windows-Linux-Docker.md

# -- Existing Docker configuration ---------------------------------------------
#
# Not needed inside the image it is building.
docker/Dockerfile
docker/compose.yaml
